{
  "version": 1,
  "area": "audio",
  "about": "The rules of the audio contract. A packet case builds a PCM16 packet of the given rate and channels and that many zero bytes and checks it against a ceiling: a packet with no data, a rate outside 8 to 192 kHz, 1 to 8 channels or a size that is not whole frames is invalid, a packet over the ceiling is too_large, and the format is judged before the size. A playback case is the same rule for the packet a playback sink takes (stereo, 48 kHz, 16 bits: a frame is 4 bytes). A limits case builds the observation limits (zero is not a case: Go reads it as the default). A devices case is a device snapshot in its wire form, and whether it is valid, under the case's device limit (default 64): only Go has such a snapshot today, and a status is complete, partial or unsupported. A select case picks a device of a kind from the standard device list (out-1, in-1, out-2, mon-1) by id, or the first of the kind when the id is empty, and expects the id or unavailable: a device of another kind or an unknown id is never a fallback. Timestamps are RFC 3339.",
